body:
I found a fun thing.

``` js
/*eslint max-statements-per-line:[2, {max:1}]*/

if (foo > 1)
  foo--;
else
  foo++;    // error here
```

``` js
/*eslint max-statements-per-line:[2, {max:1}]*/

if (foo > 1)
  foo--;
else
  foo++;    // no error!!

bar;
```

This is caused by traversal order.
Statements in block body are traversed by `for` loop in BlockStatement handler. So it addresses inside of the `if` statement after `bar;`.

I will work on this.
I think we need to rewrite the traversal way of this rule.

``` js
/*eslint max-statements-per-line:[2, {max:2}]*/

a; if (b) { c; d; }
z;
```

Currently, this `d;` should be warned, but not.
Because the traversal order of this is `a;` → `if (b)` → `z;` → `c;` → `d;`.
